# Six, investment arguments

# Look at Bull Case

1. **The Broadband River + Structural Sector Growth**: The U.S. Independent Consultants Channel is continuously capturing its share from the large Wirehouses, the largest platform of which is LPL, the most identified beneficiary. Morningstar projects an annual compound increase of about 12% in client assets over the next decade, a huge growth runway.

2. **/ The profit margin will return substantially after merger consolidation**: net interest rates will be reduced from 8.8% to 5.2% in 2025, almost entirely due to a one-time acquisition cost of $740 million. After consolidation, EPS $20.09 will be more reflective of normal profitability. On this basis, forward-looking PEs are approximately 14.7 times more likely to be valued reasonably for a large, growing company.

** The scale advantage has been continuously strengthened**: 32,000 consultants, 2.4 trillion assets, eight years of succession-based recruitment - each of which deepens the moat. Commonwealth brings about 3,000 consultants, further consolidating the leadership of the industry.

# Look at the empty logic (Bear Case)

1. ** GAAP PE nearly 29 times less expensive: GAAP profits are a reflection of real profitability if future acquisitions are in trouble and one-time costs become a continuing expense. The LPL is valued at 29 times the average value of its peers (15.4 times) and its own history (19.4 times).

** The downward trend in interest rates will systematically compress profits**: if the Fed enters an interest-reduction cycle, the cash spread income (about 10% of total income but very high profit margins) will face a structural decline. Adding the risk of cash spread litigation, the sustainability of this core profit source is questionable.

3. ** High Leveraging + Suspension Repurchase **: $7.3 billion in debt (year-on-year + 32%), moratorium on stock buybacks - typical of the aggressive acquisition. If the macro-environment deteriorates (interest rate down + market down), high leverage increases downside risk.

